---
// The bar across the top of every page: the mark, the two ways in, the theme
// toggle.
//
// It exists partly to put the logo somewhere a reader can see, and partly
// because the theme toggle was living in the home page's sidebar. Only the home
// page had it, so a reader anywhere else could not change the theme at all, and
// only the home page ran the script that applies the saved one. The second half
// of that was fixed by sharing the head; this fixes the first.
//
// Two mark files because the artwork differs by more than a background swap. The
// outlined one survives any surface, which is why the favicon uses it. On a dark
// page the plain one is better: its navy outline would sit at 1.6:1 against the
// page and do nothing but blur the silhouette.
//
// It also carries the way in, which since 2026-09-20 is two affordances rather
// than one. **There are no props and no imports here on purpose**, and the
// block above the account markup says why: everything the bar knows about a
// reader comes from one key in that reader's browser, and nothing it knows can
// come from the page it happens to be sitting on.
---

<header class="sitenav">
  <a class="brand" href="/">
    <img class="mark light-only" src="/mark-light.png" alt="" width="28" height="28" />
    <img class="mark dark-only" src="/mark-dark.png" alt="" width="28" height="28" />
    <span class="wordmark">controlbun</span>
  </a>

  {/* Contact sits in the bar rather than only at the foot of `/about/`, and the
      difference is who it reaches. A reader who bounces off the home page never
      gets to `/about/`, and that reader is the one most likely to have a
      response worth reading. It is not the "Sign in" problem in a new coat: a
      nav item is an offer, and this is an offer the build can honor today,
      because the address works and the repository is public. */}
  <nav aria-label="Sections">
    <a href="/models/">Models</a>
    <a href="/owners/">Owners</a>
    <a href="/about/">About</a>
    <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
  </nav>

  <button class="themetoggle" type="button" id="theme">Dark theme</button>

  {/* Both ways in ship in the HTML and CSS picks, off `data-who` on the root
      element, which the head script sets before first paint.

      **Nothing in this component's frontmatter feeds that choice, and that is
      the property rather than an accident of how it was written.** There are no
      props, no import of the corpus and no expression in the markup below. The
      bar cannot say a reader is somebody because the page they are on is that
      person's, which is the shape this would take the first time somebody made
      it "smarter": a submission page knows an author's handle, and a bar that
      read it would greet strangers by the name of whoever they were reading.

      **Holding a session gates nothing.** "Sign in" is a link to the page
      that explains signing in, that page links to the page that does it, and
      the page that does it is where a submission is sent from. Somebody who has
      never signed in reaches every one of those, so what changes here is which
      door is named first and nothing about who may open it.

      **Add artifact appears only when the session in this browser can still be
      renewed.** This block used to say the bar reads a remembered handle and
      that a handle never means a session exists, which was true and was the
      problem: the bar offered a way in to somebody whose session had died on
      their last reload, and the only thing behind the offer was a second
      request to sign in.

      **It points at `/submit/` and not at `/signed-in/`.** It pointed at the
      return leg while the form lived there, so pressing it during a sign-in
      reloaded that page and discarded the exchange in progress. Splitting the
      two pages is the fix, and this href is the half of it that a reader
      touches. */}
  <div class="account whenout">
    <a href="/sign-in/">Sign in</a>
  </div>

  <div class="account whenin">
    <span class="handle" id="nav-handle"></span>
    <a href="/submit/">Add artifact</a>
    <button type="button" id="signout">Sign out</button>
  </div>
</header>

{/* The receipt for a press, rather than a sentence sitting in the bar forever.
    It says the two things somebody pressing Sign out is entitled to know, and
    the second is the one they would otherwise get wrong.

    Outside the header because it is a line of prose and the bar is a flex row,
    and ignored by the search index because chrome that appears on every page
    would otherwise be a hit on every page. */}
<p class="navsaid" id="nav-said" hidden data-pagefind-ignore>
  Signed out. That deleted the session and the handle from this browser, so
  nothing here can act as you. It told nobody: the session is not revoked at the
  identity service and expires there on its own, and your Hugging Face session
  is untouched.
</p>

<script is:inline>
  // Inline and unbundled, same reason as the script in the head: no waiting on a
  // module graph for three lines of work.
  (function () {
    const button = document.querySelector("#theme");
    const root = document.documentElement;
    if (!button) return;
    const prefersDark = window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches;

    const isDark = () =>
      (root.dataset.theme || (prefersDark ? "dark" : "light")) === "dark";

    // Names the theme you would switch to, not the one you are in. This is the
    // behavior the home page already had and it is the right one for a bare text
    // button: "Light theme" while the page is light reads as a label rather than
    // as something to press.
    const label = () => {
      button.textContent = isDark() ? "Light theme" : "Dark theme";
    };
    label();

    button.addEventListener("click", () => {
      const next = isDark() ? "light" : "dark";
      root.dataset.theme = next;
      localStorage.setItem("theme", next);
      label();
    });
  }());

  // The handle, and the way to end the session it belongs to. Same shape as the
  // toggle above: a few lines of work, inline and unbundled, so nothing waits
  // on a module graph.
  (function () {
    // One key, and the only thing this file reads. It holds the session this
    // registry's identity service issued to `/signed-in/` at the exchange, plus
    // the account id, the subject and the handle it was issued for. What is
    // deliberately not in it is the provider token, the one that could write
    // repositories in somebody's own namespace, and `handshake.mjs` is where
    // that is a property of a function rather than of this comment.
    //
    // Neither service is named in this script, for the reason the head script
    // gives: this is inline, it ships on every page, and the guard that keeps
    // an identity endpoint to one page cannot tell a comment from a call.
    const HELD = "controlbun.session";
    const root = document.documentElement;
    const handle = document.querySelector("#nav-handle");
    const out = document.querySelector("#signout");
    const said = document.querySelector("#nav-said");

    // A handle only counts when the record it sits in can still be renewed.
    // Without a refresh token there is a name and no way in, which is the state
    // this key replaced.
    const handleIn = (held) => {
      const fields = held && held.refresh_token && held.user
        && held.user.user_metadata;
      return (fields && fields.preferred_username) || null;
    };

    // The head script has already set the attribute, which is what CSS reads
    // and what keeps the bar from flashing the wrong affordance. This fills in
    // the one thing an attribute cannot: the text.
    const show = () => {
      const who = root.dataset.who;
      if (handle) handle.textContent = who ? "@" + who : "";
    };
    show();

    if (out) {
      out.addEventListener("click", () => {
        // Deleting the key is the whole of signing out here, and it is a
        // smaller whole than it sounds now that the key holds a session. What
        // it does: the access token and the refresh token are gone from this
        // browser, so nothing on this origin can act as anybody. What it does
        // not: it sends no request, so the session is not revoked at the
        // identity service and lapses there on its own, and the account at
        // Hugging Face is that provider's and untouched. The receipt says both.
        //
        // No request on purpose, and it is a trade rather than an oversight.
        // Revoking would mean this bar, which is on every page, carrying the
        // identity endpoint and the publishable key on every page, and
        // `tests/test_signed_in_page.py` holds that endpoint to the one page
        // with business for it. The cost is named in `DECISIONS.md` 2026-09-20.
        localStorage.removeItem(HELD);
        delete root.dataset.who;
        show();
        if (said) said.hidden = false;
      });
    }

    // Three ways the key changes under a page that is already open:
    // `/signed-in/` finishing an exchange in this tab, the same page renewing
    // or dropping a session it restored, and another tab doing either. None is
    // common and all of them look like a bug when the bar disagrees with what
    // the reader just did.
    const again = () => {
      let held = null;
      try {
        held = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(HELD) || "null");
      } catch (unreadable) {
        held = null;
      }
      const who = handleIn(held);
      if (who) root.dataset.who = who;
      else delete root.dataset.who;
      show();
    };
    window.addEventListener("storage", again);
    window.addEventListener("controlbun:session", again);
  }());
</script>
